def summarize_clinic_batch(hb_readings):
    """Return (valid_count, anemic_count, mean_hb) skipping None and 999."""
    valid_count = 0
    anemic_count = 0
    total_hb = 0.0
    for val in hb_readings:
        if val is None or val == 999:
            continue
        valid_count += 1
        total_hb += val
        if val &lt; 11.0:
            anemic_count += 1
    if valid_count == 0:
        return (0, 0, None)
    mean_hb = round(total_hb / valid_count, 2)
    return (valid_count, anemic_count, mean_hb)


6.8.4 D. Debugging Post-Mortem: Top 3 Chapter 6 Failure Modes

Printing Instead of Returning (NoneType Cascade): Beginners routinely end a function with print(result) because it looks right in a quick test cell. The moment another function or auto-grader stores x = my_func(...), x is silently None, causing downstream TypeError failures. Always ask: “Does this function hand a value back with return?”
Calling .strip() Alone or Calling String Methods on None: In Task 6.1, raw_name.strip() trims only the outer edges—leaving "Dhaka   North" with three internal spaces—while calling .split() on None crashes with AttributeError. Pairing if raw_name is None: with " ".join(raw_name.split()) solves both traps cleanly.
Forgetting Empty-Batch and Zero-Denominator Guards: Real public health registries routinely contain clinics with population == 0, rare-disease tables with b == 0, or batches where every hemoglobin entry is None or 999. Placing early-return guard clauses (if population &lt;= 0: return None, if valid_count == 0: return (0, 0, None)) at the top of your functions makes your entire Part II–V data pipeline bulletproof!








