def merge_clinic_inventories(warehouse_a, warehouse_b):
    """Return a new dict summing shared supply keys without mutation."""
    combined = dict(warehouse_a)
    for item, qty in warehouse_b.items():
        combined[item] = combined.get(item, 0) + qty
    return combined


8.8.4 D. Debugging Post-Mortem: Top 3 Chapter 8 Failure Modes

Silent Dictionary Aliasing (combined = warehouse_a): Assigning a dictionary to a new variable name does not copy it—both names point to the exact same hash table in memory. Whenever a function merges or modifies a dictionary, always start with combined = dict(warehouse_a) (or warehouse_a.copy()) so caller data stays intact.
Counting Stray IDs Without Set Intersection: In Task 8.1, computing len(set(completed_ids)) blindly trusts every ID uploaded from field tablets, even mistyped or unenrolled IDs like "P99". Taking enrolled_set & set(completed_ids) guarantees your denominator and numerator refer to the exact same cohort.
Confusing Overwrite Merge (|, {**a, **b}) With Accumulation Merge: Python’s merge operators replace overlapping keys with the right-hand dictionary’s value—ideal for overriding config defaults, but catastrophic for summing clinic inventories or regional case counts! Always accumulate shared keys explicitly via combined[k] = combined.get(k, 0) + v.

