10.8.1 A. Concept & Execution Trace Test Key

Prints B then C (try succeeded, so except is skipped, else runs, and finally always runs).
Prints A then C (ValueError jumps to except, else is skipped, and finally always runs).
A bare except: silently swallows real programming bugs (NameError, Syntax-like runtime typos, ZeroDivisionError) and even KeyboardInterrupt, masking root causes during debugging.
Zero cases (0, 0) is a legitimate surveillance week with an undefined ratio (None), whereas negative deaths (-2) is physically impossible corrupt data that must fail loudly with ValueError.
isinstance(True, int) evaluates to True because bool subclasses int (True == 1, False == 0). Without isinstance(val, bool), boolean flags could silently masquerade as patient counts!
TypeError ('&lt;' not supported between instances of 'str' and 'float'). Always validate types before numeric range comparisons.
except ValueError: catches InvalidVitalSignError (Yes—subclasses match parent handlers). The reverse is No: except InvalidVitalSignError: will not catch a plain ValueError.
If try / except wraps the outside of the loop, the very first bad row exits the entire loop immediately, abandoning every valid record that follows it!
A constant dummy function lambda w, h: 20.0 passes any single test expecting 20.0. Testing at least two inputs with different expected BMIs (e.g., 20.0 and 25.0) exposes hardcoded shortcuts immediately.
Read from the bottom up: the final line names the exact exception type and message, and the innermost frame immediately above points to the exact expression that triggered the failure.



