# Exam Task 11.2 (35 pts)
def rank_districts_by_burden(district_records):
    """Return (district, incidence_per_100k) sorted desc rate, asc name."""
    ranked = []
    for row in district_records:
        if row["population"] &lt;= 0:
            continue
        rate = round((row["cases"] / row["population"]) * 100000, 2)
        ranked.append((row["district"], rate))
    return sorted(ranked, key=lambda item: (-item[1], item[0]))

# Exam Task 11.3 (35 pts)
def trace_exposure_depth(contact_tree, person_id):
    """Recursively return max transmission hops starting from person_id."""
    contacts = contact_tree.get(person_id, [])
    if not contacts:
        return 0
    return 1 + max(
        trace_exposure_depth(contact_tree, c) for c in contacts
    )


11.8.4 D. Debugging Post-Mortem: Top 3 Chapter 11 Failure Modes

Silent \(O(n^2)\) Bottlenecks Hidden Inside Clean One-Liners: Writing if pid in seen_list, patient_ids.count(pid), or nested for loops across patients and lab_results passes tiny 5-row tests effortlessly—then stalls on 3,000+ rows. Pre-indexing with a set or dict reduces runtime by orders of magnitude while keeping your code even cleaner.
In-Place Mutation & reverse=True Tie-Breaker Bugs: Calling patients.sort(...) mutates the caller’s list and returns None, while writing patient["hb_g_dl"] = ... silently corrupts input dictionaries. Likewise, sorted(..., reverse=True) reverses string tie-breakers. Always return fresh copies (sorted(...), dict(patient)) and negate numeric sort keys (-rate, -temp_c).
Missing Leaf Guards in Tree Recursion (KeyError / ValueError): In real contact-tracing dictionaries, terminal cases often have no key at all. Pairing contacts = contact_tree.get(person_id, []) with an explicit if not contacts: return 0 prevents both KeyError and ValueError: max() iterable argument is empty.








